38. Functions of Government and Public Choice Theory
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: I | Topic: Major economic functions of the government; public-choice theory (unanimity, majority rule, voting paradox, Arrow's theorem)
Introduction
In a mixed economy, the government performs economic roles that markets cannot or do not perform well. Musgrave's classification identifies three branches, supplemented by the legal framework. Public choice theory examines how collective decisions are actually made.
Economic functions of government
1. Legal framework: define property rights, enforce contracts, regulate competition and provide a stable monetary system. Markets cannot function without it.
2. Allocation function: correct market failures by providing public goods, controlling externalities, regulating monopolies and addressing information problems and merit goods.
3. Distribution function: adjust the distribution of income and wealth through progressive taxes, transfers and subsidies, on grounds of equity.
4. Stabilisation function: maintain full employment, price stability and growth through fiscal and monetary policy.
Public choice theory
Pioneered by Buchanan, Tullock and Downs, public choice applies economic reasoning to politics: voters, politicians and bureaucrats are self-interested. It studies how preferences are aggregated into collective choices.
Outcomes of collective choice
• Unanimity rule (Wicksell): a proposal passes only if everyone agrees. It ensures Pareto improvement and protects minorities, but decision costs are high and invite strategic holdouts.
• Majority rule: a proposal passes with over half the votes. Low decision cost, but it can impose costs on minorities and results in vote trading (logrolling). Under single-peaked preferences the median voter theorem (Black, Downs) holds: the outcome reflects the median voter's preference.
• Other rules: plurality, qualified majority, point voting.
Voting paradox
Condorcet's paradox: with three voters ranking A, B and C cyclically (A>B>C, B>C>A, C>A>B), majority voting gives A beats B, B beats C and C beats A. The collective preference is intransitive, so the result depends on the agenda order.
Arrow's impossibility theorem (1951)
No social welfare function can satisfy all of these conditions at once: unrestricted domain, Pareto efficiency, independence of irrelevant alternatives, and non-dictatorship (for three or more alternatives). The only rule meeting the others is a dictatorship. Implication: there is no perfect way to aggregate individual preferences; every voting system has flaws.
Conclusion
Government has essential economic roles, but public choice warns of government failure, so institutions and voting rules matter.